#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8743c70-1eb7-5ec4-8bb9-4c34fe99a98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357002} {:answer/id #uuid "b2899bac-97db-5f57-b481-a04587b7e4b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357003} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8c531f-b4c8-5d4a-9e6a-35f135f5ddbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357004} {:answer/id #uuid "1c93ce09-8f59-5716-b3af-4b54a575dad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357005} {:answer/id #uuid "2e938519-b510-510d-94e1-c33aec43b04b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357006} {:answer/id #uuid "edbdadbd-4697-59bf-985c-9065cd719ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\cos(xy),\\quad 0\\le xy\\le\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edbdadbd-4697-59bf-985c-9065cd719ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\cos(xy),\\quad 0\\le xy\\le\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357022} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce0ae16-eae7-50e8-8ea5-58dac8da8c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3e19cf1-1438-5521-a704-9195b3cd730a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37131" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z$ that passes through the point $P(0,1,1).$\n\nGive the full level set, including the necessary range restriction from the principal real arcsine.\n\nHint: Compute $f(0,1,1)$, then use the principal range $-\\pi/2\\le\\arcsin z\\le\\pi/2$ before taking sine." :question/worked-solution "The level surface through $P=(0,1,1)$ has level constant\n$$c=f(0,1,1)=0+\\arcsin(1)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$$\nThus $xy+\\arcsin z=\\pi/2$, or $\\arcsin z=\\pi/2-xy$. The principal real arcsine takes values in $[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$. Therefore\n$$-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}-xy\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 0\\le xy\\le\\pi.$$\nTaking sine gives $z=\\sin(\\pi/2-xy)=\\cos(xy)$. Conversely, if $0\\le xy\\le\\pi$ and $z=\\cos(xy)$, then $\\pi/2-xy$ lies in the principal arcsine range, so $\\arcsin z=\\pi/2-xy$ and $f(x,y,z)=\\pi/2$. The exact full level set is\n$$\\boxed{\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3:z=\\cos(xy),\\ 0\\le xy\\le\\pi\\}}.$$\nThe range restriction is essential. For instance $(x,y,z)=(2\\pi,1,1)$ satisfies $z=\\cos(xy)$, but $f(2\\pi,1,1)=2\\pi+\\pi/2=5\\pi/2$, not $\\pi/2$. The source's revealed answer a provides the algebraic relation but omits the restriction. Its original prompt, options, source grade and solution remain preserved; the study choice supplies the complete level set without changing the original function or point."}]}
